 "title": "Alkaline hydrolysis of an ester",
 "caption": "Hydroxide adds to the ester's carbonyl carbon to give a tetrahedral intermediate; the C=O re-forms and ethoxide leaves; ethoxide then takes the acid's proton. That last step is essentially irreversible, which is why alkaline hydrolysis goes to completion: ethanoate and ethanol.",
 "alt": "Four stages: hydroxide adding to ethyl ethanoate; the tetrahedral intermediate expelling ethoxide; ethoxide deprotonating ethanoic acid; ethanoate and ethanol.",
 "description": [
  "Stage 1: curly arrows from hydroxide to the carbonyl carbon and from C=O onto oxygen.",
  "Stage 2: the tetrahedral intermediate; arrows re-forming C=O and breaking the C–O bond to the ethoxy group.",
  "Stage 3: ethanoic acid and ethoxide; arrows from ethoxide to the acid's hydrogen and from that O–H bond onto oxygen.",
  "Stage 4: ethanoate and ethanol."
